EDG C++ front end -- C-CONVERTED FORK
=====================================

This is NOT upstream EDG.  It is EDG's own C++ sources after being run
through EDG's own C-generating back end, so that a target with a C
compiler and no C++ compiler (APExp/kencc) can build a C++ front end.

Upstream
--------
  https://github.com/edgcpp/compiler
  commit f0e30b65f  ("Implement full support for CWG 2355 (\"Deducing
  noexcept-specifiers\"). [GH #197]")

Licence
-------
  Apache License v2.0 with LLVM Exceptions.  LICENSE.txt is copied
  across verbatim from upstream and applies to everything here,
  generated or not.  The generated C is a mechanical translation of
  upstream's own sources and carries upstream's licence.

Converted
---------
  Date:  2026-10-07
  Host:  x86_64 Linux, gcc 13.3.0 (Ubuntu 13.3.0-6ubuntu2~24.04.1)
  Tool:  cpfe built from the same commit, via
           cmake --preset linux-gcc-release && ninja cpfe
         The linux-gcc-release preset's default already defines
         BACK_END_IS_C_GEN_BE=1 and DO_IL_LOWERING=1, so the DEFAULT
         build IS the C-generating one.  cpfe is 23,118,552 bytes.
         Driver: dev_tools/bin/eccp, with build/gcc-release/
         environment.sh sourced.

  src/   (75 files, from upstream src/*.c)

    eccp -S --g++ --c++14 --no_exceptions --no_rtti \
      -DBACK_END_IS_C_GEN_BE=1 -DCOMPILE_MULTIPLE_TRANSLATION_UNITS=1 \
      -DDO_IL_LOWERING=1 -DLOWER_FIXED_POINT=1 -DLOWER_IFUNC=1 \
      -DNEED_IL_DISPLAY=1 -DRECORD_BACKING_EXPRS_WITH_IL_LOWERING=1 \
      -DUSE_CMAKE_DEFINES -DHOST_HAS_FLOAT16_TYPE=0 \
      -I<build>/src/includes -I<edg>/src \
      --sys_include=/usr/lib/gcc/x86_64-linux-gnu/13/include \
      --sys_include=/usr/local/include \
      --sys_include=/usr/include/x86_64-linux-gnu \
      --sys_include=/usr/include \
      <edg>/src/<file>.c

    The -D set is upstream's own cpfe compile line, taken from
    `ninja -t commands` rather than recalled.  -I<build>/src/includes
    is where the build generates err_codes.h and err_data.h from
    src/error_msg.txt and src/error_tag.txt via util/mk_errinfo.

  lib_src/  (51 files, EDG's own C++ runtime -- new/delete, vtbl,
             static init, pure virtual, rtti.  NOT libstdc++.)

    eccp -S --building_runtime --c++23 -DUSE_STDINT_HEADER \
      -DSYSTEM_RUNTIME_HAS_IA64_THREAD_ATEXIT=0 --no_standard_includes \
      --sys_include=<edg>/include_c++ --sys_include=<edg>/include_c99 \
      --sys_include=... (as above) --g++ <edg>/lib_src/<file>.c

  util/  (7 files: decode, dettarg, edg_decode, edg_munch,
          edg_prelink, make_win_predef_macro, mk_errinfo)

    eccp -S --g++ --c++14 -I<edg>/src -I<build>/src/includes \
      --sys_include=... <edg>/util/<file>.c

  Output files are eccp's *.int.c renamed to *.c.  The only edit
  applied afterwards is a sed removing the absolute scratchpad path
  prefix from the `# NNN "file"` line markers; nothing else was
  touched by hand.

Two flags that are not upstream's
---------------------------------
  -DHOST_HAS_FLOAT16_TYPE=0  on src/.  Without it src/float_pt.c is
  the one file that will not translate:

      float_pt.c, line 253: error: identifier "_Float16" is undefined
        typedef _Float16 EDG_float16_t;

  cpfe in --g++ mode predefines __FLT16_MANT_DIG__ (mimicking gcc) but
  does not itself implement the _Float16 type, and targ_def.h:2599
  keys HOST_HAS_FLOAT16_TYPE off that macro.  Forcing it to 0 selects
  upstream's own documented fallback -- _Float16 values represented
  internally using the host `float` type.  targ_def.h guards it with
  #ifndef, so this is a supported configuration and not a patch.
  It is passed to ALL 75 files, not just float_pt.c, because
  float_pt.h and float_type.h both branch on it: one translation unit
  disagreeing would be a silent ABI split.

  EDG_GCC_VER_SCRAPE=130000 when building upstream's own runtime on
  the host (`ninja lib/libC.a`).  bases/docker/dev-env/gcc/
  edg_eccp_config defaults that variable to 140000 and then adds
  -Wno-error=return-mismatch, which is a gcc-14 flag that gcc 13 does
  not have.  Setting the variable to the real host version drops the
  four gcc-14-only -Wno-error= flags.  This affects the HOST build
  only and nothing in this directory; it is recorded here because it
  is the wall a previous attempt stopped at.  Upstream's file is not
  edited -- the variable is already the documented override.

What is here, and what is deliberately absent
---------------------------------------------
  Here:
    src/          133 generated C files in total, across the three
    lib_src/      directories.  ~2.92M lines, 160 MB.
    util/
    include_c++/  EDG's own 18 headers, copied VERBATIM, not
    include_c99/  translated.  These are what cpfe hands to the
                  programs IT compiles; they are inputs at run time,
                  not part of the front end's own build.
    LICENSE.txt

  NO HEADERS from upstream src/ are carried across, and none are
  needed: eccp's output is a whole preprocessed translation unit
  lowered to C, so each .c here is self-contained and compiles with
  no -I at all.  The upstream .h files are C++ and nothing here
  reads them.  err_codes.h / err_data.h are likewise already inlined,
  so util/mk_errinfo is carried for completeness rather than because
  a build step needs it.

  Deliberately absent, per the conversion brief:
    CMakeLists.txt, CMakePresets.json, cmake/, lib_src/cmake/,
    src/cmake/     -- build system; a Plan 9 mkfile will replace it
    dev_tools/     -- the eccp driver and cmakedef machinery; a host
                      tool, used to produce this directory, not part
                      of it
    benchmarks/, tests/, doc/, dev_annex/, bases/, nt_util/
    README.md      -- replaced by this file
    util/eccp.sh, util/make_g++_incl_paths,
      util/make_predef_macro_table  -- shell/driver, not C

  Absent because it would not translate:
    util/cfe_daemon_client.c  -- the only file in the whole tree that
    needs a real C++ standard library:

      src/cfe_daemon_common.h, line 80: catastrophic error: cannot
      open source file "cstddef"
        #include <cstddef>

    It also wants <cstring>, <cstdlib>, <cstdio>, <sys/socket.h> and
    <sys/un.h>.  It is the unix-domain-socket client for the
    compile daemon; Plan 9 has no unix sockets, so it is out of scope
    twice over.  src/cfe_daemon.c (the server side) DID translate --
    its use of those headers is behind a configuration guard.

  NO MKFILE YET.  Nothing here has been built with pcc.

State of the generated C
------------------------
  gcc -fsyntax-only over all 133 files:  0 errors, 0 warnings.
  (Verified on the host; it says nothing about kencc.)

  gcc attributes the generated C carries, and kencc has none of them:

      __attribute__((__weak__))            26712   in 66 files
      __attribute__((__always_inline__))   10035
      __attribute__((__unused__))            820
      __attribute__((__noreturn__))          267
      __attribute__((__nothrow__))           227   in 44 files
      __attribute__((__pure__))              143
      __attribute__((__malloc__))             17
      __attribute__((__alloc_size__(n)))      13
      __attribute__((__const__))               7
      __attribute__((__constructor__))         2
      __attribute__((section(".ctors.")))      1
      __attribute__((__cold__))                1

  These are NOT fixed here, by instruction.  Two different origins,
  and they want different answers:

  - __nothrow__, __always_inline__, __pure__, __malloc__,
    __alloc_size__, __const__ and most __noreturn__ come from the
    GLIBC HEADERS that were inlined into each translation unit.  On
    Plan 9 those declarations will come from APE's headers instead,
    so most of this class disappears by retranslating against APE's
    include tree rather than by defining anything away.

  - __weak__ is EDG'S OWN, and it is the load-bearing one.  It is
    emitted by src/c_gen_be.c:8806 and :11103 under the comment
    "GCC does not support COMDAT, but it does support weak, which
    provides a sufficient approximation" -- i.e. it is how the C back
    end gives vague linkage to inline functions and template
    instantiations.  26712 sites means 26712 symbols that are DEFINED
    IN MORE THAN ONE OBJECT.  Defining the attribute away would turn
    every one into a duplicate-definition link error; this is not a
    cosmetic problem.

    Upstream already has the answer for a target without weak
    symbols, and it is a BUILD-TIME CONFIGURATION rather than a
    source edit:
      * both emission sites are gated on
        `gcc_or_clang_is_generated_code_target`, and
      * src/host_envir.h:1159 and src/defines.h:389 carry
        ONE_INSTANTIATION_PER_OBJECT, the alternative model, which is
        what util/edg_prelink and util/edg_munch exist to drive --
        each template instantiation is assigned to exactly one object
        at prelink time instead of being emitted everywhere and
        merged by the linker.
    A kencc-targeted conversion should build cpfe with that
    configuration and retranslate, NOT patch 26712 attributes out of
    the C in this directory.  That has not been attempted.
